function labelLayout(cfg, cEx, cLx){
  // Both timeline labels sit on one baseline under their drop feet; if their CONFIG-box widths
  // would overlap, they shift apart symmetrically (clamped to the stage) — diagram collision
  // resolution driven only by CONFIG text + font keys.
  const wA = cfg.timelineLabelA.length * cfg.labelFontPx * cfg.labelWidthFrac;
  const wB = cfg.timelineLabelB.length * cfg.labelFontPx * cfg.labelWidthFrac;
  let ax = cEx, bx = cLx;
  const sep = () => (ax - wA / 2) - (bx + wB / 2);
  if (sep() < 0){
    const ov = -sep();
    ax = Math.min(ax + ov / 2, cfg.stageW - wA / 2 - 4);
    bx = Math.max(bx - ov / 2, wB / 2 + 4);
    if (sep() < 0){ ax = Math.min(ax - sep(), cfg.stageW - wA / 2 - 4); }
    if (sep() < 0){ bx = Math.max(bx + sep(), wB / 2 + 4); }
  }
  return { ax, bx, wA, wB };
}
function cellCenter(cfg, i, shift){
  const cellW = cfg.panelW / cfg.cols, cellH = cfg.panelH / cfg.rows;
  const row = Math.floor(i / cfg.cols), r = i % cfg.cols;
  const calX0 = (cfg.stageW - cfg.panelW) / 2;
  const cx = cfg.readingDir === "rtl"
    ? calX0 - shift + cfg.panelW - (r + 0.5) * cellW
    : calX0 - shift + (r + 0.5) * cellW;
  return { x: cx, y: cfg.panelY + (row + 0.5) * cellH, cellW, cellH };
}
function renderSVG(cfg, t){
  const st = stateAt(cfg, t);
  const P = cfg.palette;
  const cellW = cfg.panelW / cfg.cols, cellH = cfg.panelH / cfg.rows;
  const calX0 = (cfg.stageW - cfg.panelW) / 2;
  const shift = st.calShiftPxNow;
  const calX = calX0 - shift;
  const tasksX = calX + cfg.panelW + cfg.tasksGapPx + (1 - st.tasksP) * cfg.panelW * cfg.tasksTravelRatio;
  const panelBot = cfg.panelY + cfg.panelH;
  let s = "";
  s += `<svg viewBox="0 0 ${cfg.stageW} ${cfg.stageH}" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="${cfg.fontFamily}">`;
  s += `<rect x="0" y="0" width="${cfg.stageW}" height="${cfg.stageH}" fill="${P.bg}"/>`;
  s += `<g opacity="${st.gridP}">`;
## §1 — OBSERVE

- **Queue state (read this cycle):** wave RUN.md §0b/§1 (seat census at open): T243 stands at queue #108, DESCRIBED — OWED, first attempt owned by this job (A3 §3); register TECHNIQUES.md (techniques-diagrams.md §T243) concurs: DESCRIBED — OWED #108. No prior scores.md, no verdict-history line, no family sibling with filed scores for this panel machinery (T242 is the same packet's bar chart, a different mechanic, also first attempt this wave).
- **Consumer (named, §1.4):** the **DLC assembler diagram container** (and its transition-container use) owned by the Daily Language Challenge assembler lane: a panel whose elements draw on in a named order to refute a claim — the calendar is lifted as a diagram that assembles grid → strikes → drop-lines → labels, and as a transition where the fully-struck panel hands off to the next scene.

### 1.1 Catalogue mechanics (read firsthand)
- Digest §T04 (≈13–27 s): "a rounded panel labelled روزها ("Days") holds a grid of day numerals; most are struck through with diagonal slashes, two are ringed with a ⊗ mark, and dashed drop-lines connect two crossed dates to a timeline below where their labels are also struck out. A second panel (کارها, "Tasks") with empty checkboxes joins it. Draw-on order: grid → strikes → drop-lines → timeline labels." Why it works: ""after such-and-such a date it will work out" is refuted visually — the calendar itself is a record of dates that already failed."
- Style-library techniques-diagrams.md §T243: agrees on mechanics, window (P14 @ ≈13–27 s), evidence (ev/020 · ev/026). Tier DESCRIBED — OWED #108. No digest↔register disagreement.
- **Firsthand corrections to the digest prose (priced readings, binding on the build):** (1) The grid is **7 columns × 5 rows = 35 day numerals** (Persian numerals ۱–۳۵, right-to-left reading order), counted in ev/020 + census frames. (2) The "two ringed" lifecycle, CORRECTED at §2b under codex-observe (which re-read the frames cell-by-cell): a PLAIN ring first marks **day 16** (row 3, second cell from the right; census @17 s) and that SAME cell carries the ⊗ in ev/020 — the plain ring CONVERTS to ⊗ as the sweep passes it; it is not overstruck and there is NO separate day-23 ring (the builder's first reading misattributed the cell — cured here). The second ring marks **day 34** plain by 19 s and converts to ⊗ only in the final state (25 s). (3) In the final state ALL 35 cells are marked — day 35 (bottom-left) carries a diagonal slash in ev/026 (codex-observe's count: 35 marked incl. the two ⊗ cells); the builder's first reading ("day 35 never struck") is cured: the final strike target is 35, with day 35's slash landing in the final phase. (4) The timeline + star figure + dashed arc are ALREADY present at the first calendar frame (17 s) with ONE drop-line (from day 16) and its label UNSTRUCK; the second drop-line (from day 34), the first label's strike, and the second label arrive by 19 s. The digest's draw-on order (grid → strikes → drop-lines → timeline labels) is the CLAIM the build enforces for its own phases; the source's pre-assembled timeline/figure is named as set, not technique, in §2.2 deferrals. (5) The window head (13–17 s) is T242's bar chart and the tail (≥26.5 s) dissolves into the next scene (tiny population figures, panels dimming) — both are departures tabled in §2.3, not T243 lifecycle. (6) The figure's chest star CRACKS at 26 s — the next beat's opening glyph, deferred (§2.2). (7) Entrance anchor (codex-observe, firsthand): at source 16.5 s the calendar is partially drawn with first-row strikes visible; by 17.0 s it is assembled — the build's grid-phase expansion (§2.3 departure b) is priced against this anchor.

### 1.2 Evidence frames viewed firsthand
### 2.2 Construction choice
**DOM/SVG single-stage scene (chosen):** one SVG; background rect; calendar group (panel rect + title tab + per-cell numeral text + per-cell strike line + ring marks) translating with calShiftP; tasks group (panel + title + checkbox rows) joining at tasks phase; timeline group (line + ticks + two labels with strike crosses + figure + dashed arc); dashed drop-lines from ring cells to the timeline. All geometry from `stateAt(cfg,t)`, a pure function evaluable in node; `window.__T243` exposed.
**Figure + arc disposition (§8 size discipline, codex-plan-review item 5 — applied in part with reason):** the figure and arc are SET, carried at minimal geometry (figure: head circle + body/leg path + chest star, from figureCx/figureScale; arc: one dashed quadratic path whose opacity is state-derived) — they are present because both drop-lines land on the timeline the figure stands on and the arc's disappearance is a census state change (CP12000) the checkpoints assert; a DETAILED figure reconstruction (proportions, star-crack glyph) is deferred as a later attempt's ornament, and no checkpoint asserts figure detail beyond presence + arcOpacity.
Rejected: Canvas 2D — loses per-element ink measurement the A6 fit leg needs. CSS keyframes — phase state not evaluable as a pure function (A11 receipts impossible). Photographic/generated imagery — approaches the specimen; the technique is the draw-on order, carried by geometry.
**Named deferrals (lawful, priced):** (1) Window head (source 13–17 s, T242's bars) — outside this technique; the allocation discards it (departure table §2.3). (2) The figure's chest-star crack at 26 s — the next beat's glyph; the build holds the final state. (3) The tail transition (≥26.5 s: dimming panels, tiny population figures) — next scene, not built. (4) The "perhaps…" annotation at 23 s — caption-adjacent scene text, not technique; not built. (5) Persian numeral glyphs + Persian label texts — the build renders CONFIG Latin strings (numerals derived from firstDay, labels as CONFIG text); the technique is the order + marks, not the specimen's language — the swap test re-labels freely, which is the parameterization proof. (6) The source's calendar entrance is unobserved (inside the 15→17 s gap): the build's grid phase is an ALLOCATION expanding that entrance so the named order reads — priced in §2.5, departure-tabled in §2.3. (7) The figure's lateral drift in the source (it tracks the calendar's shift and recentres by the final state) is simplified to a FIXED figureCx — the drop-lines land on the timeline at CONFIG-derived x positions in both readings; the drift is staging, not technique.

### T243 — Calendar-of-days panel with crossed-out dates (P14 T04) ◆ — OWED #108
- Tier: **DESCRIBED — OWED #108**
- Mechanics: a rounded panel holds a grid of day numerals; most are struck through with diagonal slashes, two are ringed, and dashed drop-lines connect two crossed dates to a timeline below whose labels are also struck out; a second panel with empty checkboxes joins it. Draw-on order: grid → strikes → drop-lines → timeline labels — "after such-and-such a date it will work out" is refuted by the calendar itself. (Named draw-on order — OWED.)
- Feeds: diagram · transition — P14 @ ≈13–27 s — ev/020-calendar-days.jpg, ev/026-calendar-tasks-crossout.jpg

### T246 — Resource bars over standing figures (day-2 depletion scene) (P14 T07)
- Tier: **DESCRIBED — not owed**
- Mechanics: three figures stand in a row; above each, a tall thin bar outline stands nearly empty, with a small filled segment at the base of the middle bar; below, an empty rounded report panel waits. The bars do not animate down on screen — depletion is shown as an already-low state, held: day 2's losses are inventory, not events. (Held state, no motion — not owed, priced from the packet's own mechanics.)
- Feeds: diagram — P14 @ ≈49–58 s — ev/050-day2-resource-bars.jpg
49:3b. **Pre-wave Jev gate:** `guards/jev-prewave.md` judges the build record after legs 1–3 and before the wave (leg 4): FIRE-WAVE spends the wave; HOLD returns the build to Execute with its classification and the wave moves to its next item. **Receipt readiness check (Amendment A12, 2026-11-18 — performed by the builder BEFORE the payload is assembled):** every A11 checkpoint in the build-record carries its executable receipt — the harness command or run, the CONFIG expression evaluated, and the raw result from the build under review; for a CONFIG timing swap the receipt shows the changed evaluation instant. A checkpoint with a claim but no receipt returns the record to the builder before the call is spent. **Receipt-index reconciliation (Amendment A13, 2026-11-18):** the build-record carries a RECEIPT INDEX enumerating (a) every guards.md command applicable to the item and (b) every A11 assertion for the item; before the payload is assembled the builder reconciles the index against the applicable guard commands, the item's planned A11 assertions, and — for a completion or re-attempt — the assertion sets established in the filed prior harnesses and their amendments, and, for census receipts, against the build's expected CONFIG keys (nested/array keys included). Every index line carries its executable receipt or an explicit NOT-DEMANDED line whose disposition cites the governing plan or guard; a missing expected line, an unsupported exclusion, or a line with neither returns the record to the builder. A receipt is valid only if it carries the evidence its assertion requires: for each CONFIG-derived input, the CONFIG expression and its evaluated value; for each check, the raw observed result. A populated receipt lacking that evidence returns the record to the builder on the same form. This check is a readiness check for the mandatory call, not a gate on it: the call still fires at its step on every cycle, and a cycle in which it did not fire prints INCOMPLETE under the standing firing ruling. The deliverable-class judgment for this job IS the five-lens scoring wave (leg 4, defined in `guards/codex-scoring-wave.md`) — no separate jev-verify file exists, by design: the wave's per-lens verdicts + leg 5's mechanical consolidation are the deliverable gate, and a second Jev over the same deliverable would duplicate them.
123:Every item maps each Observe census checkpoint to an executable own-check assertion and its CONFIG-derived evaluation instant. The build-record identifies the checkpoint, the assertion, the CONFIG expression, and the evaluated result. jev-prewave returns ASSERTED-CHECKS if any checkpoint lacks this mapping, any evaluation instant uses hardcoded defaults, or execution evidence is absent. A CONFIG timing swap must move the corresponding assertion's evaluation instant. Demonstrations: practice T153 (timing 45→92) + T162 (timing 64→96), practice Cycle 019 · recreation Wave 25, all four items (timing lenses 92/96/96/100, no timing-stuck item, against Wave 22's allocation-vs-census timing failures at 68/64). Enforcement lives in jev-prewave.md §2 leg (3), amended in the same landing.
126:1. **Receipt form (operative step: §5 leg 3b's readiness check, referenced from §3 step 4).** Every A11 assertion in a build-record files its EXECUTABLE RECEIPT beside the claim — the harness command or run, the CONFIG expression evaluated, and the raw result from the build under review; for a CONFIG timing swap the receipt shows the changed evaluation instant. A9.4 already required raw output; this clause names the receipt's three parts and wires the check into the prewave step itself. A build-record whose assertions carry claims without receipts is incomplete at the builder's own gate: jev-prewave is not spent on it (the call's firing obligation is unchanged — the readiness check precedes payload assembly, and an unfired call still prints the cycle INCOMPLETE).
